<!doctype html>
<html lang="{{ request.locale.iso_code }}" class="sbs-html">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
    <meta name="theme-color" content="#0B0D12">
    <link rel="canonical" href="{{ canonical_url }}">
    {%- comment -%}
      Referrer policy, set through the meta element because Shopify's storefront
      does not expose response headers to the theme. `strict-origin-when-cross-origin`
      is already the browser default when no policy is sent, so this codifies the
      current behaviour and makes the intent auditable rather than changing what
      browsers do today.

      Permissions-Policy has no meta equivalent — it is a response header only —
      so it cannot be set from here at all. That is recorded as a known gap in
      docs/seo/SECURITY-HEADERS.md rather than left to look like an oversight.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    <meta name="referrer" content="strict-origin-when-cross-origin">
    <link rel="preconnect" href="https://cdn.shopify.com" crossorigin>

    {%- if settings.favicon != blank -%}
      <link rel="icon" type="image/png" href="{{ settings.favicon | image_url: width: 32, height: 32 }}">
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- comment -%}
      On the homepage Shopify's `page_title` falls back to the myshopify domain
      when the Online Store > Preferences title is unset, and there is no API to
      set that. So the homepage uses theme settings instead; every other
      template uses its own resource title as normal.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if template.name == 'index' -%}
      {%- assign seo_title = settings.home_seo_title | default: shop.name -%}
      {%- assign seo_desc = settings.home_seo_description | default: shop.description -%}
    {%- else -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        A literal en dash, not &ndash;. og:title runs this through a filter,
        which would turn the entity's ampersand into &amp; and print the
        entity source into the tag.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        No suffix when the title already carries the brand in either spelling:
        a product's SEO title may end "| SiteBuilderStack", and appending
        "– Site Builder Stack" would name the shop twice in one title.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- assign brand_compact = shop.name | remove: ' ' -%}
      {%- capture seo_title -%}
        {%- if page_title contains shop.name or page_title contains brand_compact -%}
          {{ page_title }}
        {%- else -%}
          {{ page_title }} – {{ shop.name }}
        {%- endif -%}
      {%- endcapture -%}
      {%- assign seo_desc = page_description -%}
      {%- if seo_desc == blank and template == blank -%}
        {%- comment -%} policy pages: Shopify supplies no page_description {%- endcomment -%}
        {%- assign seo_desc = page_title | append: ' for ' | append: shop.name | append: '. Digital products, delivered by download.' -%}
      {%- endif -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        Shopify generates the "All" collection with no description, so
        /collections/all shipped with no meta description at all. Built from the
        live catalogue rather than hard-coded, so it cannot go stale when a
        product is added or a price changes.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- if seo_desc == blank and template.name == 'collection' -%}
        {%- assign n_products = collection.all_products_count -%}
        {%- if n_products == 1 -%}
          {%- assign product_noun = ' downloadable product: ' -%}
        {%- else -%}
          {%- assign product_noun = ' downloadable products: ' -%}
        {%- endif -%}
        {%- assign seo_desc = 'Claude Code systems for building, ranking and converting websites. ' | append: n_products | append: product_noun | append: 'the workflows, prompts and audits, run in your own terminal.' -%}
      {%- endif -%}
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- comment -%}
      Tag archives on the blog reuse the blog's own title and description, so
      every /blogs/guides/tagged/* URL shipped an exact duplicate of the index's
      title tag. They are also thin: each lists a subset of articles that the
      index already lists in full, and adds no text of its own.

      Two changes, both deliberate: a distinct title and description so the
      pages are honestly labelled for anyone who lands on one, and
      `noindex, follow` so they stop competing with the index while still
      passing link equity through to the articles.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if template.name == 'blog' and current_tags != blank -%}
      {%- assign tag_label = current_tags | join: ' and ' -%}
      {%- capture seo_title -%}{{ tag_label }} guides – {{ blog.title }} – {{ shop.name }}{%- endcapture -%}
      {%- capture seo_desc -%}Every {{ blog.title }} article tagged {{ tag_label }}. The full set of guides is at {{ blog.title }}.{%- endcapture -%}
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- comment -%}
      Page two onwards of a paginated list shipped the same title and
      description as page one, which is a duplicate across two indexable URLs.
      Both now carry the page number. The pages stay indexable and
      self-canonicalising, which is what Google asks for — pointing every page
      at page one hides the articles that only appear later in the list.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- if current_page > 1 -%}
      {%- assign seo_title = seo_title | strip | prepend: '' -%}
      {%- capture seo_title -%}{{ seo_title | strip }} – Page {{ current_page }}{%- endcapture -%}
      {%- capture seo_desc -%}Page {{ current_page }} of {{ seo_desc | strip_html | strip }}{%- endcapture -%}
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- comment -%}
      A page number past the end of a list still returns 200 on Shopify with an
      empty result set, which is a thin page a crawler can reach by guessing.
      BLOG_PER_PAGE must match `paginate blog.articles by N` in
      sections/sbs-blog.liquid; tests/run-all.sh asserts the two agree, because
      a silent drift here would put a real paginated page out of the index.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- assign BLOG_PER_PAGE = 48 -%}
    {%- if template.name == 'blog' and current_page > 1 -%}
      {%- assign last_page = blog.articles_count | divided_by: BLOG_PER_PAGE -%}
      {%- assign remainder = blog.articles_count | modulo: BLOG_PER_PAGE -%}
      {%- if remainder > 0 -%}{%- assign last_page = last_page | plus: 1 -%}{%- endif -%}
      {%- if current_page > last_page -%}
        {%- assign blog_page_empty = true -%}
      {%- endif -%}
    {%- endif -%}

    <title>{{ seo_title | strip }}</title>

    {%- if template.name == 'blog' and current_tags != blank -%}
      <meta name="robots" content="noindex, follow">
    {%- elsif blog_page_empty -%}
      <meta name="robots" content="noindex, follow">
    {%- endif -%}

    {%- comment -%}
      There is deliberately no blanket noindex on collections.

      /collections/frontpage used to be a duplicate of the homepage sitting in
      the sitemap, and was noindexed to keep it out of the index. It has since
      been unpublished from the Online Store channel instead, which removes it
      from the storefront and the sitemap outright — a better answer than a
      noindexed URL Google still has to fetch to discover the directive.

      A blanket rule here would silently hide any collection published later,
      so it is gone. templates/collection.json and sections/sbs-collection.liquid
      stay, so a future collection renders in the site's own design rather than
      falling through to the purchased theme.
    {%- endcomment -%}

    {%- if seo_desc != blank -%}
      {%- comment -%}
        escape_once, not escape. Shopify hands back `page_description` already
        HTML-escaped, so escaping it again turns "SEO & Audit" into
        "SEO &amp;amp; Audit" in the served markup. og:description was fixed
        for this in an earlier cycle and this line was missed, so any page whose
        description contained an ampersand shipped the double-escaped version.

        escape_once is also correct for the homepage, whose description comes
        from a theme setting and is NOT pre-escaped: a raw "&" still becomes
        "&amp;" exactly once.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      <meta name="description" content="{{ seo_desc | strip_html | escape_once }}">
    {%- endif -%}

    {% comment %}
      Open Graph and Twitter Card. Built here rather than in a snippet so the
      landing layer has no dependency on the parent theme's social meta.
    {% endcomment %}
    {%- comment -%}
      escape_once, not escape. Shopify's page_title arrives already escaped for
      a resource whose title contains an ampersand, so `escape` produced
      &amp;amp; in og:title and twitter:title — which social previews and link
      unfurlers render as the literal text "&amp;". The <title> tag was
      correct because it prints page_title unfiltered; only the meta tags were
      wrong, which is why it survived a year unnoticed.

      escape_once escapes a bare & and leaves an existing entity alone, which
      is right for this string: it is a mix of Shopify's escaped page_title and
      the theme's own unescaped shop.name.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- assign og_title = seo_title | strip | escape_once -%}
    {%- assign og_desc = seo_desc | strip_html | escape_once -%}
    <meta property="og:site_name" content="{{ shop.name | escape }}">
    <meta property="og:url" content="{{ canonical_url }}">
    <meta property="og:title" content="{{ og_title }}">
    <meta property="og:description" content="{{ og_desc }}">
    <meta property="og:type" content="{% if template.name == 'product' %}product{% elsif template.name == 'article' %}article{% else %}website{% endif %}">
    {%- comment -%}
      image_url returns a protocol-relative URL, so prepend https (not http).
      Guarded on the image object itself: a settings value with no matching
      image_picker in settings_schema stays a plain string, and image_url then
      emits a Liquid error straight into the tag.
    {%- endcomment -%}
    {%- assign og_image = nil -%}
    {%- if template.name == 'product' and product.featured_media != blank -%}
      {%- assign og_image = product.featured_media -%}
    {%- elsif template.name == 'article' and article.image != blank -%}
      {%- assign og_image = article.image -%}
    {%- elsif settings.share_image != blank -%}
      {%- assign og_image = settings.share_image -%}
    {%- endif -%}
    {%- if og_image != blank -%}
      <meta property="og:image" content="{{ og_image | image_url: width: 1200 | prepend: 'https:' }}">
      <meta property="og:image:width" content="1200">
      <meta property="og:image:alt" content="{{ og_title }}">
    {%- endif -%}
    <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image">
    <meta name="twitter:title" content="{{ og_title }}">
    <meta name="twitter:description" content="{{ og_desc }}">

    {{ content_for_header }}

    <link rel="stylesheet" href="{{ 'sbs.css' | asset_url }}">
    <script src="{{ 'sbs.js' | asset_url }}" defer></script>

    {% render 'sbs-schema' %}
  </head>

  <body class="sbs sbs-template-{{ template.name }}">
    <a class="sbs-skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a>

    {% sections 'sbs-header-group' %}

    <main id="main" tabindex="-1">
      {{ content_for_layout }}
    </main>

    {% sections 'sbs-footer-group' %}
  </body>
</html>
